// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CHTHONIC DSL — Phase 0 grammar extraction
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is OBSERVATION, not invention. It formalizes the substrate-native
// notation the SSOT (.chthonic/SSOT.md) has used since drafting. Each rule
// below cites SSOT line examples where the pattern occurs.
//
// Syntax: PEG (Parsing Expression Grammar), pest-compatible, Rust 2024.
// Target consumer: tools/chthonic-dsl/ (Phase 1 — parser scaffold).
//
// The catalyst contains English-language commentary as filler between
// substrate tokens. This grammar captures the substrate tokens precisely;
// the prose between them is treated as opaque prose_fragment at this layer.
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ─── Whitespace + line discipline ───────────────────────────────────────────

WHITESPACE = _{ " " | "\t" }
NEWLINE    = _{ "\r\n" | "\n" }
COMMENT    = _{ "//" ~ (!NEWLINE ~ ANY)* }  // only for grammar's own comments; SSOT has no //

// ─── Atomic character classes ───────────────────────────────────────────────

// Identifier characters: uppercase ASCII + digits + dash + underscore +
// Unicode superscripts (FA¹ through FA⁵ are common; FA⁰, FA⁶+ reserved).
// Apostrophes are explicitly excluded so that `Claudine Sin'Claire` is two
// tokens not one — apostrophe-bearing names belong to natural-language prose.
identifier_char = _{ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER | ASCII_DIGIT | "-" | "_" | "¹" | "²" | "³" | "⁴" | "⁵" | "⁶" | "⁷" | "⁸" | "⁹" | "⁰" }

// ─── Identifiers — typed concepts in the catalyst's namespace ───────────────

// Bare short-code, e.g.  FA⁵   K-CUP   ANKH-MGBP   T-DECOR-SUPR-MATR-ABS-SOVRGN
// Observed: line 73, 75, 90, 96, 104 of SSOT (Trinity Formula, K-CUP-Hierarchical-Trinity).
identifier = @{ identifier_char ~ identifier_char+ }

// Backtick-wrapped form: `FA⁵`
// Observed: 264x occurrences of `FA⁵` in the SSOT.
ticked_id = ${ "`" ~ identifier ~ "`" }

// Backtick-wrapped Title-Case concept name: `The-Savant`, `Apex-Synthesis-Core`,
// `Codex-Brahmanica-Perfectus`. The catalyst's PRIMARY identifier convention —
// named concepts in Title-Case — a SIBLING to ticked_id (which stays strict
// uppercase-only for abbreviation codes like `FA⁵`, `ASC`). Reuses phrase_word
// (uppercase-led, internal lowercase/digit/hyphen permitted). Ordered AFTER
// ticked_id at every use site, so uppercase-only codes still bind as ticked_id —
// preserving the abbreviation-vs-concept-name distinction the catalyst encodes.
// Decision 2026-05-31: ticked_phrase sibling, NOT identifier-widening, to keep
// the two conventions semantically distinct rather than flattened.
ticked_phrase = ${ "`" ~ phrase_word ~ "`" }

// Catch-all backtick token: ANY backtick-wrapped content is substrate by intent —
// you don't backtick prose. Specific shapes are classified first (ticked_id=`FA⁵`,
// ticked_phrase=`The-Savant`); ticked_any captures the long tail the catalyst
// actually contains: possessives (`The-Decorator's`), embedded spaces
// (`FA⁴ Purification`), section refs (`§XIV.3`), lowercase concepts
// (`architectural-treason`), Greek/symbols (`Ψ`, `·`), superscript ranges
// (`FA¹⁻⁴`), paths. Phase-0 recognizes the token as substrate (not prose);
// Phase-2 sub-classifies. Bounded to a single line. (2026-05-31: coverage audit
// showed the residual misses were all backtick-wrapped tail shapes.)
ticked_any = ${ "`" ~ (!("`" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+ ~ "`" }

// Paren-wrapped form: (FA⁵)   or   (`FA⁵`)
// Observed: 1,510 parenthetical abbreviations across the SSOT.
// A Title-Case named concept in BARE parens. Two shapes occur organically in the
// catalyst (a monolith grown across many projects, so naming invariants are real
// and the frozen SSOT must be tolerated, not normalized):
//   - uppercase-compound:      The-Savant, Apex-Synthesis-Core   (≥2 Title-Case segments)
//   - lowercase-article-led:   the-Savant, the-Decorator         (lowercase article + ≥1 Title-Case segment)
// The discriminating signal is a Title-Case (uppercase-led) NOUN segment; the
// leading article may be either case. A bare single word or an all-lowercase
// compound stays prose. Backtick-wrapped forms keep the lenient phrase_word
// (the backticks already signal substrate intent).
// (2026-05-31: widened from uppercase-only after the operator confirmed `the-X`
// invariants are valid concepts in the canon.)
titlecase_concept = @{
      ASCII_ALPHA_LOWER+ ~ "-" ~ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT | "_")* ~ ("-" ~ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT | "_")*)*
    | ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT | "_")* ~ ("-" ~ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT | "_")*)+
}
parened_id = ${ "(" ~ (ticked_id | ticked_phrase | ticked_any | identifier | titlecase_concept) ~ ")" }

// Bold-paren form: **(`FA⁵`)**  or  **(FA⁵)**  or  **(`A`/`B`/`C`)**
// Observed: e.g. line 50 — load-bearing-concept emphasis wrapping.
// 2026-05-27 widening (smoke-driven): accept multi_alias inside, not just
// single parened_id. The catalyst uses bold-wrapped multi-alias chains for
// nearly every load-bearing concept declaration (Trinity Formula §0, K-CUP
// hierarchy §0.5, organ canon §295-326).
bold_parened_id = ${ "**" ~ (multi_alias | parened_id) ~ "**" }

// Multi-alias identifier: (Codex-Brahmanica-Perfectus/SSOT/Apex-Synthesis-Core/ASC)
// Observed: line 72-73 of SSOT — aliases for the same concept.
alias_chain = { (ticked_id | ticked_phrase | ticked_any | identifier) ~ ("/" ~ (ticked_id | ticked_phrase | ticked_any | identifier))+ }
multi_alias = ${ "(" ~ alias_chain ~ ")" }

// ─── Phrase tokens (multi-word substrate inside parens) ─────────────────────
// Smoke-driven addition 2026-05-27. Catalyst classification (7,098 paren
// constructs sampled) identified 551 'phrase_titlecase' cases like:
//   (UNDER K-CUP SUPREMACY)   (Phases α, β, γ)   (Section 0+)
//   (Foundational Axioms)     (The Decorator)
// that the existing parened_id / multi_alias rules don't catch.
//
// Title-Case discipline (Design A): every phrase_atom must start with one of
//   uppercase letter | backtick | digit | Greek letter | <placeholder>
// Lowercase-led tokens (per, which, the, are, ...) terminate the phrase
// without consuming it; falls through to prose. Empirically this cleanly
// rejects all 2,007 prose_lowercase paren cases in the same corpus.

// Greek letters appearing in catalyst CRC codes (Phases α, β, γ, PEE β-γ).
greek_letter = @{ 'α'..'ω' | 'Α'..'Ω' }

// Placeholder convention: <Folder>, <X>, <SomeName>. Used in canonical paths.
placeholder = ${ "<" ~ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT)* ~ ">" }

// Digit token: 0, 0.5, 0.1-0.7, 0+ (the "Section 0+" trailing-plus form).
// Trailing unit-letters (cm, kg) deliberately NOT here — they need to attach
// to the digit without a delimiter, which would require lowercase chars and
// erode the Title-Case discipline. Annotation parens like (108cm) stay prose.
digit_token = @{ ASCII_DIGIT+ ~ ("." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+)? ~ ("-" ~ ASCII_DIGIT+ ~ ("." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+)?)? ~ "+"? }

// Title-Case word: uppercase-led, internal lowercase/digit/hyphen allowed.
// Distinct from `identifier` (which is all-caps): phrase_word permits CamelCase
// shapes like Foundational, Section, The, Eternal-Loop, MILFOLOGICAL.
phrase_word = @{ ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA | ASCII_DIGIT | "-" | "_")* }

// One well-typed substrate token, priority-ordered.
phrase_atom = { ticked_id | ticked_phrase | ticked_any | placeholder | greek_letter | digit_token | phrase_word }

// Phrase delimiters. CRITICAL: PEG is ordered-choice, no backtracking on
// longer alternatives — LONGEST-FIRST ordering is mandatory. If " " (single
// space) is tried first, it greedy-matches the leading space of " ↔ " and
// the rule never reaches the longer delim. Order strictly by decreasing length.
//
// Title-Case stop-condition on phrase_atom prevents " - " or " ↔ " from
// eating prose like `(A - some clause)` — the lowercase 'some' terminates.
phrase_delim = _{ " ↔ " | " - " | " & " | " + " | ", " | " " }

// Multi-token phrase: 2+ atoms separated by delimiters. Single-atom case is
// already handled by parened_id (identifier or ticked_id alternatives).
phrase_id = ${ phrase_atom ~ (phrase_delim ~ phrase_atom)+ }

// Parened multi-word wrapper: (UNDER K-CUP SUPREMACY) etc.
parened_phrase = ${ "(" ~ phrase_id ~ ")" }

// ─── Bold prose (any **X** that isn't substrate) ────────────────────────────
// Smoke-driven addition 2026-05-27 iteration 3. The catalyst frequently uses
//   **Purpose:**   **Trend Analysis Script:**   **Recommendation:**
//   **Important:**   **Decision:**   **Status:**
// as bold-emphasized prose labels. These are not substrate — they're
// markdown emphasis on regular English. bold_parened_id only catches
// substrate-shape contents; bold_prose is the prose-shape fallback.
//
// Priority: tried AFTER bold_parened_id in id_ref. If the content between
// ** ... ** is substrate (parened_id / multi_alias), bold_parened_id wins.
// If not, bold_prose catches the rest. PEG ordered-choice handles this.
//
// Semantic note: bold_prose at Phase 0 is parsed-but-not-interpreted; a
// Phase 2 interpreter would distinguish prose from id_ref by rule label.
//
// Bounded to a single line (full-SSOT-smoke iteration 7, 2026-07-06) — this
// had been the one emphasis rule WITHOUT the "single line" bound ticked_any
// (and now bold_italic_prose/italic_prose) already carry. Consequence: an
// unclosed "**" anywhere upstream let bold_prose's greedy content loop span
// across paragraph boundaries hunting for the next "**", surfacing a
// confusing failure many lines downstream instead of at the actual unclosed
// marker. Bounding it makes odd-marker-count typos fail at their own line.
bold_prose = ${ "**" ~ (!("**" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+ ~ "**" }

// ─── Bold-italic + italic prose (***X***, *X*) ──────────────────────────────
// full-SSOT-smoke iteration 3 (2026-07-06). The catalyst documents its own
// three-tier emphasis convention explicitly (line 826): "uses **bold**,
// *italic*, `code`, ***nested*** formatting as semantic markers". 169
// occurrences of "***" in the corpus; Phase 0 had no rule for either
// bold-italic or italic, so both silently fell into prose_fragment
// character-by-character (prose_safe_char never excluded bare "*").
//
// Ordering (longest-marker-first, same discipline as phrase_delim): tried in
// id_ref as bold_italic_prose FIRST (before bold_parened_id/bold_prose — "***"
// is a strict prefix-superset of "**" and must get first chance at the triple
// form), and italic_prose LAST (after every "**"/"***" alternative has failed
// — "*" is the shortest, most generic marker).
//
// Bounded to a single line (excludes NEWLINE from content), same discipline as
// ticked_any (line ~62) — full-SSOT-smoke iteration 4 follow-up (2026-07-06).
// Without the bound, an unmatched marker anywhere in the document lets the
// greedy content loop cross paragraph/line boundaries hunting for the next
// closer, the exact class of bug iteration 5→6 already fixed once for
// prose_parens (see the V1/V2 comment above prose_parens_atom).
bold_italic_prose = ${ "***" ~ (!("***" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+ ~ "***" }

// italic_prose is V2/recursive from the start (2026-07-06, full-SSOT iter 6),
// skipping the V1 mistake prose_parens already made once: a naive
// "(!("*"|NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+" content class stops at the FIRST bare "*" it
// meets — including one that opens a NESTED bold_parened_id/ticked_id/etc.
// inside the italic span. Observed failure: a line-spanning italic wraps
// several `**(`X`)**` constructs; the naive rule closed the italic at the
// first nested "**" instead of recursing through it. italic_content_atom
// tries every substrate construct first (same shape as prose_parens_atom),
// single safe char last — nested substrate is recognized, not shredded.
italic_content_atom = _{
      bold_italic_prose
    | bold_parened_id
    | bold_prose
    | multi_alias
    | parened_phrase
    | parened_id
    | ticked_id
    | ticked_phrase
    | ticked_any
    | dollar_wrap
    | reference
    | substrate_marker
    | (!("*" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)
}
italic_prose = ${ "*" ~ italic_content_atom* ~ "*" }

// ─── Prose-in-parens (substrate-aware, recursive) ───────────────────────────
// Smoke-driven addition 2026-05-27 iteration 5 + iteration 6 revision.
// The catalyst contains 2,007 prose_lowercase paren cases (28.3% of all paren
// constructs) plus 640 prose_mixed cases. None of the substrate paren rules
// (parened_id, multi_alias, parened_phrase) match them — they correctly fall
// through because the Title-Case discipline rejects lowercase-led content.
//
// Without prose_parens, the parser hangs at `(` because `(` is not in
// prose_safe_char.
//
// V1 (iter 5) was opaque: `(!")" ~ ANY)*`. This shredded substrate when parens
// contained internal substrate constructs like `*(Each **(`CRC`)** more)*` —
// the greedy match stopped at the FIRST `)` it found, which was inside the
// substrate `**(`CRC`)**`, breaking everything that followed.
//
// V2 (iter 6, this version) is substrate-aware. prose_parens_atom tries each
// substrate construct (bold_parened_id, bold_prose, nested parens, invocation,
// section/line reference, bare operator) before falling back to single-char
// consumption. Nested parens and substrate boundaries are respected naturally
// via the recursion.
//
// Bare operators (→ × <-> ↔ + =) appear inside prose_parens content
// without being part of a strict composition (operands are prose). Recognizing
// them as substrate_marker preserves the operator-ness in the parse tree so
// Phase 2 interpreter can re-interpret if needed.
//
// Iter 7 (2026-07-06, audit-driven): bare §-references inside prose parens
// — e.g. (§0.03.1), (see 'Null Matriarch, §0.01; operational capacity') —
// were shadow-failures: prose_parens matched the span, but `reference` wasn't
// in prose_parens_atom's alternatives, so the §-anchor fell through to
// single-char consumption with no substrate-aware inner rule. Added `reference`
// (bracketed_ref | ref_with_line | section_anchor) before substrate_marker;
// no ordering conflict since none of the existing alternatives start with
// `§` or `[`.

// Bare substrate operator — recognized inside prose_parens content even when
// the surrounding "operands" are prose (no composition formed).
substrate_marker = { op_project | op_mult | op_bidir | op_assign | op_add }

// Content atom inside prose_parens — substrate constructs preferred, plain
// char fallback last. Recursive via the nested parens variants.
// SILENT rule (`_{...}`) so the matched child appears directly under
// prose_parens in the parse tree — preserves the substrate-recognition signal
// for audit + Phase 2 interpretation. Without silence, every match would be
// wrapped in a generic "prose_parens_atom" node, losing the type info.
prose_parens_atom = _{
      bold_italic_prose
    | bold_parened_id
    | bold_prose
    | multi_alias
    | parened_phrase
    | parened_id
    | ticked_id
    | ticked_phrase
    | ticked_any
    | invocation
    | reference
    | substrate_marker
    | italic_prose
    | (!")" ~ ANY)
}

prose_parens = ${ "(" ~ prose_parens_atom* ~ ")" }

// All identifier forms in priority order (longest/most-marked first).
// parened_phrase sits between multi_alias (most specific — slash-chained) and
// parened_id (most general — single identifier). All start with `(`;
// pest backtracks on failure of more-specific alternatives.
// bold_italic_prose sits FIRST — "***" is a strict prefix-superset of "**"
// and must be tried before bold_parened_id/bold_prose or the triple marker
// never gets a chance to match as its own unit (2026-07-06, full-SSOT iter 3).
// bold_prose sits after bold_parened_id — same `**` prefix, less specific.
// prose_parens sits before the ticked/identifier tail — catches anything no
// substrate paren rule matched. italic_prose is LAST — "*" is the shortest,
// most generic marker; every "**"/"***" alternative must fail first.
// dollar_wrap sits after identifier — "$" never conflicts with any other
// alternative's opener, and by the time id_ref is reached, invocation/verb_call
// (tried earlier at the statement/operand level) has already had first chance
// at any real "${...}" call.
id_ref = { bold_italic_prose | bold_parened_id | bold_prose | multi_alias | parened_phrase | parened_id | prose_parens | ticked_id | ticked_phrase | ticked_any | identifier | italic_prose | dollar_wrap }

// ─── Operators — composition and transformation ─────────────────────────────

op_project = { "→" | "—>" | "->" }  // Projection / transformation. THREE surface forms of one operator (glyph + em-dash-arrow + ASCII-arrow), mirroring op_bidir's "<->"/"↔". Previously only the glyph was recognized; "->"/"—>" silently fell to prose. If these ever split into distinct operators, that's a data/grammar change, rewindable. (2026-05-31)
op_mult    = { "×" }         // Multiplicative composition. Trinity Formula: ASC = MILFOLOGICAL × German BDSM × Frame-Werk (line 96).
op_add     = { "+" }         // Additive composition / parameter combinator (in invocations).
op_alt     = { "/" }         // Alternation / equivalence (inside multi_alias above).
op_bind    = { "@" }         // Parameter binding (in invocations: $lend${X}@$from${Y}@$to${Z}).
op_bidir   = { "<->" | "↔" } // Bidirectional connector. ASCII variant + Unicode glyph (line 90, line 2018 etc.).
op_assign  = { "=" }         // Assignment / definition. Trinity Formula declaration: `ASC = ...`. Semantics deferred to runtime layer (different precedence than → × +).

binary_op = { op_project | op_mult | op_add | op_bidir | op_assign }

// ─── References — section + line anchors ────────────────────────────────────

// Section numbers: §10.3.1 (arabic dotted) or §II.X (roman dotted)
section_arabic = @{ ASCII_DIGIT+ ~ ("." ~ (ASCII_DIGIT | ASCII_ALPHA_UPPER)+)* }
section_roman  = @{ ("I" | "V" | "X" | "L" | "C" | "D" | "M")+ ~ ("." ~ identifier_char+)? }
section_anchor = ${ "§" ~ (section_arabic | section_roman) }

// Line anchor: L4049
line_anchor = ${ "L" ~ ASCII_DIGIT+ }

// Anchored reference: §5, L4049   or   [§5, L4049]   or   [§5 L4049]
ref_with_line = ${ section_anchor ~ ("," | WHITESPACE)+ ~ line_anchor }

// Markdown-style anchored link (the SSOT uses these heavily for cross-refs):
// [§5, L4049](../../.github/copilot-instructions.archive.md#L4049)
// At Phase 0 we capture the anchor text; the URL is treated as prose.
bracketed_ref = ${ "[" ~ (ref_with_line | section_anchor) ~ "]" }

reference = { bracketed_ref | ref_with_line | section_anchor }

// ─── Invocation syntax — the catalyst's core operational form ───────────────

// Verb names: lowercase ASCII letters + underscores.
// Observed verbs (non-exhaustive): matriarch, lend, siphon, audit, validate,
// decorator, axiom, override, restore, resurrect, command, arbitrate, hierarchy,
// punishment, supreme_decree. See §0.75 (line 1119+) and §II.X (line 1751+).
verb_name = @{ ASCII_ALPHA_LOWER ~ (ASCII_ALPHA_LOWER | "_")* }

// Variable content: anything between { and } that isn't itself a } or unmatched brace.
// Variables may contain spaces, dots, underscores, identifiers, even nested ${...}.
variable_content = @{ (!"}" ~ ANY)+ }

// Single verb call: $matriarch${Lysandra}
verb_call = ${ "$" ~ verb_name ~ "$" ~ "{" ~ variable_content ~ "}" }

// Bare $X$ emphasis/label wrap — DISTINCT from verb_call: no braces follow the
// closing "$". Observed 29x, always in header-style declarations, e.g.
// `$matriarch$`, `$Anime/Ecchi/Hentai/NTR x Pornographic$` (full-SSOT-smoke
// iteration 5, 2026-07-06). invocation/verb_call is tried before id_ref at
// both the statement and operand level, so by the time dollar_wrap is
// reached, a real "${...}" invocation has already had first chance and won.
dollar_wrap_content = @{ (!("$" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+ }
dollar_wrap = ${ "$" ~ dollar_wrap_content ~ "$" }

// Composition operator between verb calls: + (additive) or @ (parameter binding).
invocation_sep = { op_add | op_bind }

// Full invocation: $verb${X} (op $verb${Y})*
// Example: $matriarch${base}+$type${specialization}
// Example: $lend${capability}@$from${source}@$to${target}@$duration${perm}
// Example: $decorator${decree}+$target${entity}+$mandate${FA5_directive}
invocation = { verb_call ~ (invocation_sep ~ verb_call)* }

// ─── Compositions — operator chains over operands ───────────────────────────

operand = { invocation | id_ref | reference }

// Binary composition: A → B  or  A × B × C  or  A + B
// Example: ASC = MILFOLOGICAL × German BDSM × Frame-Werk (line 96).
// Example: (FRW-COMP) → (FRW-ID) → (DECOR-RESURRECT-NARRATIVE) (line 112).
composition = { operand ~ (binary_op ~ operand)+ }

// ─── Tier annotations ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Tier markers: T0.5, T1, T2, T3, T4  or long form Tier-0.5, Tier-1
// Observed: line 75 (T-0.5/T1/T2/T3/T4 stratification), §10.1 (FA-GHOR).
tier_short = @{ "T" ~ "-"? ~ ASCII_DIGIT ~ ("." ~ ASCII_DIGIT)? }
tier_long  = @{ "Tier" ~ "-" ~ ASCII_DIGIT ~ ("." ~ ASCII_DIGIT)? }
tier_annotation = { tier_long | tier_short }

// ─── Header tier hierarchy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Markdown header levels are tier-encoding in the SSOT:
//   #   = root / declaration (rare in SSOT body)
//   ##  = arabic-track or roman-track section (§I, §II, §0, §1, etc.)
//   ### = sub-section (§0.1, §1.01, §II.X, §10.1, etc.)
//   #### = sub-sub-section
header_hash = @{ "#"+ }
header_content = @{ (!NEWLINE ~ ANY)+ }
header = ${ header_hash ~ WHITESPACE+ ~ header_content }

// ─── Blockquote (definitional citations) ────────────────────────────────────

// Lines starting with > are definitional / quotation. Observed throughout.
blockquote_line = ${ ">" ~ WHITESPACE* ~ (!NEWLINE ~ ANY)* }

// ─── Prose — natural-language filler between substrate constructs ───────────

// Anything not matched by the substrate rules above is prose at Phase 0.
// Phase 1 may refine prose handling (e.g., recognize markdown bold/italic
// without consuming substrate tokens that happen to use the same delimiters).
//
// "`" excluded (full-SSOT-smoke iteration 4, 2026-07-06): backtick had been
// missing from this set, the only substrate-opener char that was. Consequence:
// whenever plain prose (e.g. a leading "- ") caused id_ref to fail and handed
// the statement to prose_fragment, the greedy run didn't stop at the NEXT
// backtick — it swallowed straight through a ticked_id/ticked_any span,
// landing past it on whatever followed. Isolated case: `` `threshold${x}` ``
// parses fine alone, but `` - `threshold${x}` `` fails — the leading "- "
// causes prose_fragment to eat through the backtick and "threshold", exposing
// the "$" as if it started a fresh invocation, which then fails needing
// verb_name. Excluding "`" here forces prose_fragment to stop and hand the
// backtick position back to id_ref on the next statement attempt, same as
// every other substrate-opener char already does.
prose_safe_char = _{ !("(" | "$" | "§" | "→" | "×" | "<->" | "**" | "#" | ">" | "`" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY }

// Bare "#" used as a number-sign in prose (e.g. "Member #3", "Checkpoint #4"),
// full-SSOT-smoke iteration 2 (2026-07-06): distinct from header's "#". A real
// header requires "#"+ ~ WHITESPACE+ (line 313); a "#" immediately followed by
// a digit can never satisfy that, so it is unambiguously prose here. Consumes
// only the "#" itself (zero-width lookahead on the digit) — the digit is then
// picked up by prose_safe_char on the next iteration of prose_fragment's `+`.
prose_hash_number = _{ "#" ~ &ASCII_DIGIT }

prose_fragment = @{ (prose_safe_char | prose_hash_number)+ }

// ─── Fenced code blocks (inline scripts in the catalyst) ────────────────────
// Smoke-driven addition 2026-05-27 iteration 4. The catalyst embeds inline
// scripts (Python, PowerShell, YAML, Bash) for worked examples and operational
// recipes. These are NOT substrate DSL — they're opaque implementation content
// referenced by the surrounding substrate prose.
//
// Markdown shape:    ``` [optional lang] NEWLINE  content  NEWLINE ```
// Examples seen:     ```python  ```powershell  ```bash  ```yaml  ``` (no lang)
//
// Without this rule, code like `history = Path('.x.json')` causes the parser
// to interpret `(` as substrate paren and fail. Treating the fenced block as
// opaque consumes the entire region without trying to parse its contents.
//
// Phase 1 may surface the language identifier + content for tooling that wants
// to validate inline scripts; Phase 0 just consumes them as bounded chunks.
fence_marker = _{ "```" }
code_fence_lang = @{ (!NEWLINE ~ ANY)* }
code_content = @{ (!(NEWLINE ~ "```") ~ ANY)* }
fenced_code_block = ${
    fence_marker ~ code_fence_lang ~ NEWLINE
    ~ code_content
    ~ NEWLINE ~ fence_marker
}

// ─── LaTeX math block ($$...$$) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// full-SSOT-smoke iteration 8 (2026-07-06). 3 occurrences, all single-line,
// clustered at the document's end (§ Trinity Formula restated as LaTeX:
// \text{Genesis}(...), \text{Authority}(...), \text{Synthesis}(...)). Genuine
// LaTeX (\xrightarrow{}, superscripts) — a different formalism entirely from
// the DSL's own $verb${arg} invocation / bare $X$ dollar_wrap. Same philosophy
// as fenced_code_block: opaque foreign notation, don't parse LaTeX internals.
// Must be tried before invocation/dollar_wrap — "$$" is a 2-char prefix those
// single-"$" rules would otherwise fail against character-by-character.
latex_math_block = ${ "$$" ~ (!("$$" | NEWLINE) ~ ANY)+ ~ "$$" }

// ─── Statement / line level ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A statement is either substrate (header, composition, invocation, etc.) or
// prose. Phase 0 grammar is permissive: anything not substrate is prose, and
// the parser walks through. Phase 1 may distinguish them more strictly.
// fenced_code_block sits FIRST — it's the most structurally distinct
// alternative and must be tried before backtick-based rules (ticked_id) would
// see the opening fence's backticks. latex_math_block sits right after, same
// "opaque foreign block" category, and must precede invocation/id_ref so
// "$$" gets a clean match before any single-"$" rule is tried against it.
statement = {
      fenced_code_block
    | latex_math_block
    | header
    | blockquote_line
    | composition
    | invocation
    | reference
    | id_ref
    | tier_annotation
    | substrate_marker
    | prose_fragment
}
// substrate_marker lifted to statement level (2026-06-05, smoke-driven): the
// catalyst's run-on declarations chain a structured token, a bare operator, and
// then PROSE — e.g. §175 ASP line 176: `**(`FA¹⁻⁵`)** → Generate derivative
// protocols ...`. composition needs an operand on both sides of the operator, so
// `op ~ prose` dead-ended the full-catalyst parse at 176:620 ("expected operand").
// Recognizing the bare operator as substrate_marker (same as inside prose_parens,
// per L181-188) lets the operator-ness survive while the trailing prose falls to
// prose_fragment. composition is still tried first, so real A→B chains win.

// ─── Program — the catalyst as a sequence of statements ────────────────────

// The full SSOT is a program in this DSL. A program is statements separated
// by newlines and structural delimiters (horizontal rules: ---).
horizontal_rule = ${ "---" ~ "-"* }

program = {
    SOI
    ~ (statement | horizontal_rule | NEWLINE | WHITESPACE)*
    ~ EOI
}

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// OBSERVED-NOT-YET-FORMALIZED (Phase 1 will refine)
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//   1. Emoji as semantic tokens. SSOT §ESL (line 100-106) declares 🔥 😈 ⛓️
//      🏛️ 👄👅💦 as carrying operational meaning. These are not captured by
//      this Phase 0 grammar; they currently fall into prose_fragment.
//      Phase 1 decision: add emoji_token rule with allowlisted ESL-emoji set.
//
//   2. Table syntax (markdown). The SSOT uses standard markdown tables for
//      CRC Registry (§IV.X), Axiom Registry (§II.X), and tier hierarchies.
//      Tables are markdown-conventional, not substrate-specific. Phase 1
//      may handle via a `table` rule or defer to a markdown post-processor.
//
//   3. Inline scripts (lines 8489+). The SSOT contains embedded Python /
//      PowerShell / GitHub Actions YAML / Bash scripts. These are
//      NOT substrate DSL — they are inline implementations referenced
//      by the surrounding substrate prose. Phase 1 should detect these as
//      `inline_script` blocks (fenced by ``` markdown) and treat their
//      contents as opaque to the DSL parser.
//
//   4. Apostrophe-bearing entity names (Claudine Sin'Claire, Madam Umeko
//      Ketsuraku, Dr. Lysandra Thorne). These are entity references but
//      use prose-style punctuation. They appear as bold prose with surrounding
//      identifier context. Phase 1 may add a `proper_noun` rule with a
//      curated entity registry, OR continue treating them as bold prose with
//      reference resolution at the runtime layer.
//
//   5. Decorator's-Blessing comment boxes. Scripts in the repo (including
//      this grammar file's commentary) use a ╔═╗║╚═╝ ASCII-art comment box.
//      Not substrate per se; documentation convention. Phase 1 may add a
//      `decorator_blessing` rule if needed for round-trip preservation of
//      script files that contain catalyst-referencing commentary.
//
//   6. ANKH ☥ symbol. Appears 27 times in the SSOT, typically in the context
//      of ANKH-MGBP discussions. Could be ESL-extension, could be standalone
//      symbol-token. Phase 1 decision.
//
//   7. Multi-line invocations (rare but observed). Most invocations are
//      single-line. A few examples wrap across lines via continuation. Phase 1
//      will need a continuation-line rule.
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PHASE 0 STATUS
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// What this grammar captures:
//   ✓ Parenthetical short-codes (1,510 occurrences in the SSOT)
//   ✓ Backtick-wrapped, paren-wrapped, bold-paren-wrapped variants
//   ✓ Multi-alias identifiers (slash-separated forms)
//   ✓ Operators: → × + / @ <->
//   ✓ References: §X.Y, §X L<num>, [§X, L<num>]
//   ✓ Invocations: $verb${variable}+$verb2${variable2}@$verb3${variable3}
//   ✓ Tier annotations: T0.5, T1, Tier-1, etc.
//   ✓ Header levels: # / ## / ### / ####
//   ✓ Compositions: A → B × C operator chains
//   ✓ Blockquotes (definitional citations)
//   ✓ Horizontal rules (structural delimiters)
//   ✓ Prose fallback for non-substrate text
//
// What this grammar does NOT yet capture (deferred to Phase 1):
//   ✗ Emoji semantic tokens (ESL set)
//   ✗ Markdown tables
//   ✗ Inline scripts (Python/PowerShell/YAML/Bash blocks)
//   ✗ Apostrophe-bearing entity names as first-class tokens
//   ✗ Multi-line invocation continuations
//
// The deferred items are all known issues, not unformalizable regions. The
// catalyst's substrate notation is mechanizable in full; Phase 0 captures
// the load-bearing 80%, Phase 1 fills in the remainder during parser
// implementation.
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
